    "value": "<p>How Zimmermann customisation works on top of the base Salesforce PWA Kit template. For general naming/testing/styling conventions, see the parent <a href=\"https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/261750785\">Development Standards</a> page.</p><h2>The overrides pattern</h2><p>This project is a <strong>slim overrides project</strong> on top of <code>@salesforce/retail-react-app</code> v10.</p><p>The base template lives in <code>node_modules/@salesforce/retail-react-app/</code>. It cannot and must not be edited — changes there would be wiped out by the next <code>npm install</code>. All Zimmermann customisations live in <code>overrides/app/</code>, which the PWA Kit module resolution layer checks before falling back to the base template. For example:</p><ac:structured-macro ac:macro-id=\"339ea5d2-6651-4988-8385-425496080109\" ac:name=\"code\" ac:schema-version=\"1\"><ac:parameter ac:name=\"theme\">Default</ac:parameter><ac:parameter ac:name=\"language\">none</ac:parameter><ac:plain-text-body><![CDATA[node_modules/@salesforce/retail-react-app/app/components/product-tile/index.jsx\n↑ base template — never touch this\n\noverrides/app/components/product-tile/index.tsx\n↑ Zimmermann override — this is what runs in production]]></ac:plain-text-body></ac:structured-macro><p>If a file exists in <code>overrides/app/</code>, it replaces the base template version entirely. If it does not exist in <code>overrides/app/</code>, the base template version runs.</p><h2>Overriding vs Creating a new file</h2><h3>A. Overriding a base component</h3><p>Use this when changing a component that already exists in the base template (e.g. <code>product-tile</code>, <code>header</code>, <code>checkout</code>).</p><ol><li><strong>Copy the file</strong> — copy the base file to the mirrored path under <code>overrides/</code>, convert it to TypeScript, and add a temporary <code>console.log</code> to confirm the override is rendering:    <ac:structured-macro ac:macro-id=\"b95f2adb-8327-46d1-84be-798c0d064634\" ac:name=\"code\" ac:schema-version=\"1\"><ac:parameter ac:name=\"theme\">Default</ac:parameter><ac:parameter ac:name=\"language\">bash</ac:parameter><ac:plain-text-body><![CDATA[cp node_modules/@salesforce/retail-react-app/app/components/product-tile/index.jsx \\\n  overrides/app/components/product-tile/index.tsx]]></ac:plain-text-body></ac:structured-macro></li><li>Convert the file to TypeScript (<code>.js</code> → <code>.ts</code>/<code>.tsx</code>), and update import references within the new file.</li><li>Run <code>npx tsc --noEmit</code> to confirm no TypeScript errors.</li></ol><p>To test the override is working: add <code>console.log('[overrides] product-tile/index.tsx')</code> near the top, start the dev server, open the relevant page, and confirm the log fires in the browser console. Remove the <code>console.log</code> and make functional changes only after the override is confirmed rendering.</p><p><strong>Keep the filename identical</strong> (kebab-case) — overrides of Salesforce files keep the upstream filename exactly.</p><h3>B. Creating a new Zimmermann-specific component</h3><p>Use this when building something that doesn't exist in the base template (e.g. a \"Back In Stock\" button, a loyalty points badge).</p><p>New custom components go under <code>overrides/app/components/custom/</code>, e.g.:</p><ac:structured-macro ac:macro-id=\"f652887a-4d00-4c83-8484-7bf411f2a1b9\" ac:name=\"code\" ac:schema-version=\"1\"><ac:parameter ac:name=\"theme\">Default</ac:parameter><ac:parameter ac:name=\"language\">none</ac:parameter><ac:plain-text-body><![CDATA[overrides/app/components/custom/backInStockButton/\n  index.tsx              ← the component\n  index.stories.tsx      ← Storybook story (required)\n  index.test.ts          ← Vitest (required if the component has non-trivial logic)]]></ac:plain-text-body></ac:structured-macro><p>Naming: <code>camelCase</code> for Zimmermann-authored folders and files (e.g. <code>backInStockButton/</code>, <code>useWishlist.ts</code>, <code>formatPrice.ts</code>).</p><h2>Branch first</h2><p>Every change goes on a feature branch — never directly on <code>develop</code> or <code>main</code>.</p><ac:structured-macro ac:macro-id=\"c451c62d-05d4-42c4-a754-e2af3c524d0f\" ac:name=\"code\" ac:schema-version=\"1\"><ac:parameter ac:name=\"theme\">Default</ac:parameter><ac:parameter ac:name=\"language\">bash</ac:parameter><ac:plain-text-body><![CDATA[git checkout develop\ngit pull origin develop\ngit checkout -b feature/SFCC-123_short-description]]></ac:plain-text-body></ac:structured-macro><p>Branch naming: <code>feature/{JIRA-KEY}_{short-description}</code>. Full branching model: see the <a href=\"https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/304840733\">Branching Guide (GitFlow)</a> page under Development Standards &amp; Tooling.</p>",
    "format": "storage"
  },
  "emoji": "1f680"
}"}

{"result":"{
  "metadata": {
    "id": "400195600",
    "title": "04.71 Writing User Stories for Components",
    "type": "page",
    "created": "",
    "updated": "",
    "url": "https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/400195600",
    "space": {
      "key": "SFCC",
      "name": "Adobe SFCC"
    },
    "version": 2,
    "attachments": [],
    "content": {
      "value": "### How to write user stories for the Zimmermann build\n\n#### Overview\n\nUser stories are organised across two levels: component epics and page epics. Each serves a different purpose and audience.\n\n---\n\n#### Level 1 — Component epics\n\nThis is the build story for each individual component. It covers what the component does, its variants and configurations, accessibility requirements, and any error or empty states.\n\n**There are two types of component, and this determines how many stories you write:**\n\n**PWA Kit only** (e.g. Button, Textfield, Dropdown, Cart Item)  \nThese are functional UI components with no content authoring required. Write one story, in the PWA Kit epic only. No Contentful story is needed.\n\n**Contentful + PWA Kit** (e.g. Banner, Editorial Cards, Footer, Navigation)  \nThese components have content that an editor manages. Write two stories:\n\n* One in the **Contentful epic** — covering the content model, authoring fields, configuration options and validation rules\n* One in the **PWA Kit epic** — covering how the component renders and responds to what Contentful delivers\n\n> A Contentful component always needs a PWA Kit story too, because PWA Kit has to render it. A PWA Kit-only component does not need a Contentful story.\n\n---\n\n#### Level 2 — Page epics\n\nThis is the experience story for a specific page (e.g. PDP, PLP, Checkout). Rather than focusing on individual component builds, these stories describe how components work together to deliver a user journey.\n\nWrite these from the customer's perspective — for example, on the PDP: a customer selects a size using the size selector, selects a colour using the colour attribute component, and adds the item to their bag using the add to cart component.\n\nThese stories should not repeat the component build detail from Level 1. They are about the interaction between components and the end-to-end experience on that page.\n\n---\n\n#### Quick reference\n\n| Level | Epic | Focus | Written from |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | PWA Kit component | Build, variants, accessibility | Developer perspective |\n| 1 | Contentful component | Content model, authoring, field config | Merchandiser perspective |\n| 1 | PWA Kit (render) | Rendering Contentful output | Developer perspective |\n| 2 | Page (e.g. PDP) | How components work together | Shopper perspective |",
      "format": "markdown"
    }
  }
}"}

