      "name": "Adobe SFCC"
    },
    "version": 12,
    "attachments": [],
    "content": {
      "value": "Jira Story Workflow for SFCC Project\n====================================\n\nThis page explains the expected Jira workflow for user stories in the SFCC project, including what each status means and the actions expected from the team while a story is in that state.\n\nProposed Change: To Do -> In BA -> Ready for Review (PO sign off) -> In Tech Design (once solution is clear, conversation with developers/QA, address open points) -> Ready for Dev\n\nHandovers: BA → SA\n\n| **Stage in board** | **Jira Workflow Status** | **Assignee** | **Expected actions during this state** | **Automations** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Backlog | **To Do** | BA | Capture the story request, business need, acceptance criteria, dependencies, and any supporting links or assets. Business Analyst begins drafting the story for review. |  |\n| Refinement | **In Refinement** | BA | Business Analyst writes and refines the story - including acceptance criteria, designs, business rules, dependencies and any supporting links or assets. Story is not ready for technical review until BA work is complete. |  |\n| Refinement | **Ready for Review** | BA - PO - BA | Story is complete from a business and requirements perspective. Product Owner reviews and signs off before the story is passed to technical design. |  |\n| Refinement | **In Tech Design** | BA - SA | Define and document the technical solution. Confirm impacted SFCC cartridges, services, jobs, integrations, data model changes, configuration updates, API contracts, security and performance considerations, and deployment approach. Developers and QA are involved at this stage to address open points and confirm the approach. Story is sized and estimated here. Capture design decisions, dependencies, risks, and any required technical tasks in Jira. The Definition of Ready Checklist must be completed and up to date before a story can be moved to Ready for Development. | The Definition of Ready Checklist must be completed and up to date before a Story can be moved to Ready for Development \\* [Automation Rule](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/jira/software/projects/SFCC/settings/automation#/rule/019eb9c3-9d77-7d1b-bc07-5737c112576d) |\n| Blocked | **Blocked** | The person working on the story | The team raises it immediately in standup, assigns an owner, and add details on the User Story before the transition completes. The SM logs it in the RAID log, follows up daily, and escalates to the Delivery Lead if there is no response within 24 hours. Meanwhile, the developer picks up a stretch story. The SM verifies resolution before applying the Unblock transition. |  |\n| Ready for Development | **Ready for Development** | SM | Confirm the story is assigned, sprint committed, and unblocked. Developers validate branch strategy, environment access, SFCC instance availability, API credentials, test data, and any required design or configuration inputs. | Ticket is moved to In Progress if commits are raised for a ticket  \\* [Automation rule](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/jira/software/projects/SFCC/settings/automation#/rule/019eba1f-0258-7369-a471-ff1c40cb8660) |\n| Development | **In Progress** | Developer | Implement the change in SFCC, including code, configuration, templates, controllers, jobs, services, or Business Manager updates as required. Keep the Jira story updated, raise blockers early, and link commits or pull requests. |  |\n| Development | **Code Review** | Dev Lead | Submit the pull request and request review from the appropriate technical reviewers. Review code quality, SFCC best practices, security, performance, test coverage, and alignment with acceptance criteria. Address review feedback before moving forward. | Once PR is Merged ticket status is updated to Ready for QA \\* [Automation rule](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/jira/software/projects/SFCC/settings/automation#/rule/019eba20-3e99-7ebb-aa1c-40bf5f071972) |\n| Testing | **Ready for QA** | QA | Deploy the change to the QA environment, provide test notes, impacted areas, configuration steps, and any sample data needed. Confirm the build is stable and the story is ready for QA validation. |  |\n| Testing | **In QA** | QA | QA validates the story against acceptance criteria, regression impact, browser/device coverage, storefront behavior, Business Manager configuration, integrations, and edge cases. Defects are logged and linked to the story.  If passed, QA to coordinate with developer to deploy to SIT |  |\n| Testing | **In SIT** | QA | Change is deployed SIT environment and QA to validate the story against acceptance criteria, regression impact, browser/device coverage, storefront behavior, Business Manager configuration, integrations, and edge cases. Defects are logged and linked to the story. |  |\n| Testing | **Ready to deploy to UAT** | Developer | Developer that developed the story should deploy to UAT environment |  |\n| UAT | **Ready for UAT** | PO | Confirm QA sign-off and prepare the story for business validation. Provide release notes, testing instructions, credentials if needed, and any known limitations for Product Owner or business stakeholders. |  |\n| UAT | **In UAT** | PO | Business stakeholders validate the story in the UAT environment. Product Owner confirms the feature meets business expectations and acceptance criteria. Any feedback or defects are documented in Jira. |  |\n| Ready for Release | **Ready for Release** | Dev Lead | Confirm UAT approval, release readiness, deployment steps, rollback plan, feature toggles, configuration changes, and release notes. Ensure the story is included in the correct release package. |  |\n| Done | **Done** |  | Confirm production deployment and post-release validation. Close the story after acceptance criteria are met, documentation is updated, and any follow-up tasks or defects are created if required. | The Definition of Done Checklist must be completed and up to date before a Story can be moved to Done  \\* [Automation rule](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/jira/software/projects/SFCC/settings/automation#/rule/019eb9c5-0168-7d8d-9862-193fb921066a) |\n\nGeneral expectations\n--------------------\n\n* Keep Jira fields, assignee, priority, sprint, labels, components, and fix version up to date.\n* Update the story whenever the scope, estimate, acceptance criteria, or dependencies change.\n* Link related defects, pull requests, design references, test evidence, and release documentation.\n* Move the story to the next status only when the expected actions for the current status are complete.\n* Use comments to make decisions, blockers, handoffs, and approvals visible to the wider team.\n* Issues to be marked with a Flag when its blocked and progress cannot continue due to missing requirements, dependencies, environment issues, access problems, or external decisions. Clearly document the blocker, owner, required action, and target resolution date.",
      "format": "markdown"
    }
  }
}"}

